WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.412
Three constructs keep divisions apart.

2
00:00:02.412 --> 00:00:04.788
First, separate address spaces.

3
00:00:04.788 --> 00:00:09.970
When a lab's private range overlaps
headquarters and renumbering isn't practical,

4
00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:14.849
IPAM needs a separate routing domain,
so the same addresses can exist twice.

5
00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:20.625
Infoblox calls these network views;
Universal DDI uses IP spaces.

6
00:00:20.625 --> 00:00:22.313
Two consequences follow.

7
00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:26.227
In NIOS, a DHCP server serves
only one network view,

8
00:00:26.227 --> 00:00:31.287
so a division with overlapping
space needs its own DHCP servers.

9
00:00:31.287 --> 00:00:34.660
And NIOS doesn't enforce
uniqueness across views;

10
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:40.417
Universal DDI's IPAM Federation does,
recording which blocks are globally unique,

11
00:00:40.417 --> 00:00:44.776
which are delegated, and which
overlap deliberately behind NAT.

12
00:00:44.776 --> 00:00:47.953
Second, DNS views, which
give different answers

13
00:00:47.953 --> 00:00:51.050
to different clients by
source address or key.

14
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.826
The first matching view wins,

15
00:00:52.826 --> 00:00:56.600
so an external catch-all placed
first hides the internal view.

16
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.085
Cloud services have their own versions;

17
00:00:59.085 --> 00:01:02.850
an Azure private zone answers
only the networks linked to it.

18
00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.650
Third, tags for ownership:
division, cost center, site.

19
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:13.325
Infoblox calls them extensible
attributes; Universal DDI calls them tags.

20
00:01:13.325 --> 00:01:16.763
They drive filtering and
reports, not permissions.

21
00:01:16.763 --> 00:01:21.026
And a required tag is enforced
only in the graphical interface.

22
00:01:21.026 --> 00:01:24.865
Objects created through the API
or a CSV import can skip it,

23
00:01:24.865 --> 00:01:28.625
so the automation has to
enforce the division tag itself.

24
00:01:28.625 --> 00:01:33.825
One trap: in NIOS, report data isn't
filtered by object permissions.

25
00:01:33.825 --> 00:01:39.041
Granting a division the reporting app
shows it agency-wide DNS and DHCP data,

26
00:01:39.041 --> 00:01:42.000
so share dashboards per division instead.
